r form a Pung of B3 or B6 (but they only have one each)\n\nAlternatively, maybe the user is aiming for a hand with multiple Pungs or specific patterns. Let's check the scoring patterns. For example, if the user can form four Pungs and a pair, that would be \"All Pungs\" (8 points) or if they have more. But with the current tiles, they have one Chow and need three more melds. If they can form three Pungs and a pair, that would be four melds plus a pair.\n\nLooking at the hand, the user has two J1s (possible pair). Then, if they can form three Pungs, that would be possible. But the user has only one of each tile except J1. So forming Pungs would require getting three of the same tile. For example, the user has one W1, one W2, etc. Not enough for Pungs unless they can get more. So maybe the user is trying to form Chows instead.\n\nAnother possibility is that the user is aiming for a \"Mixed Straight\" (8 points), which requires three Chows in different suits for 1-2-3, 4-5-6, 7-8-9. The user already has T5-T6-T7 (7-8-9 in T suit). If they can form T1-T2-T3 and W4-W5-W6, that would be three Chows. Plus the pair of J1s. That would be a Mixed Straight.\n\nBut the user has T1 and T2. If they can get T3 (unshown T3:4), and W4 and W5 are already in hand, needing W6 (unshown W6:2), then that's possible. However, the user can't draw, so they have to discard a tile that doesn't block these possibilities. So which tile to discard?\n\nLooking at the current hand, the user has W1, W2, W4, W5. To form W4-W5-W6, they need W6. If they discard W5, they lose the chance to form that Chow. Similarly, if they discard W4, they can't form it. But if they keep W4 and W5, and hope to get W6, that's better. The same with T1 and T2; if they discard T1 or T2, they can't form T1-T2-T3.\n\nSo the user needs to keep W4 and W5, T1 and T2, and J1s. The tiles that are not part of these potential Chows are W1, B3, B6, T5 (already in the meld), etc. The user has T5 in hand, but since they already have a