te, so the T5 in the meld is already used. The T5 in my hand is a separate tile. So if I discard T5, it's the one in my hand. But since I have a meld using T5, maybe it's safe to discard? Or maybe not. If someone can use it for a meld. For example, if they have T4 and T6, they can form a Chow. But I have T6 in my hand. Not sure.\n\nPlay T6: Similar to T5. The T6 in the meld is part of the meld, but the T6 in my hand is a separate tile. Discarding it might be useful if someone can use it. For example, if someone has T5 and T7, they can form a Chow. But I have T5 in my hand. So if I discard T6, they could form a Chow. But the meld already has T5 and T7. Not sure.\n\nPlay T2: Discard T2. If someone has T1 and T3, they can form a Chow. But I have T3. If they have T1, they can Chi it. The game history shows the player across played T1. Maybe they have T1. So discarding T2 could be risky.\n\nPlay W4: Discard W4. If someone has W3 and W5, they can form a Chow. I have W5. So if they have W3, they can Chi it. The player across has a Chi of W3. So they might have W3. So discarding W4 could be risky.\n\nPlay W2: Discard W2. If someone has W1 and W3, they can form a Chow. I have W1. So if they have W3, they can Chi it. The player across has a Chi of W3. So they might have W3. So discarding W2 is risky.\n\nPlay J3: Discard J3. If someone has two J3s, they can form a Pung. But I have one, and there are two left. So if I discard J3, someone might have two and form a Pung. But the next player played J3 in their history. So maybe they have one. But not sure.\n\nNow, considering all this, which tile is the safest to discard? The one that gives others the least chance to form a meld.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\n- Discarding W1: Risk of someone Chi with W2 and W3 (player across has Chi W3, so possible).\n- Discarding T3: Risk of someone Chi with T2 and T4 (next player has Peng T4, so possible).\n- Discarding W2: Risk of someone Chi with W1 and W3 (player across has Chi W3, so possibl