lso, the user's hand has T2 and T3. If they can get T4, they can form a Chi. But T4 is in unshown with 1 left. If they discard T3, they lose that chance. So maybe it's better to keep T3. \n\nSo the valid actions that don't interfere with potential melds are Play W6, Play B6, Play T2 (if T2 is part of a potential Chi with T3 and T4). \n\nIf they discard T2, they can still keep T3. If they can get T4, they can form T2-3-4. But if they discard T2, they lose that possibility. So maybe it's better to keep T2. \n\nSo the best options are Play W6 or Play B6. \n\nBetween these, which one is better? Let's see. If they discard W6, they have W2 and W4 left. These can't form a Chi. If they discard B6, they have B2, B3, B4 for the Chi. So forming the B Chi is more valuable. Therefore, it's better to discard W6. \n\nSo the answer would be Play W6. This allows the user to keep the B2, B3, B4 for a Chi, and the T2, T3 for a potential Chi if T4 is available. Discarding W6 doesn't interfere with any needed melds and removes a tile that can't form part of a Chi.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The best move is to discard W6 to preserve the potential for forming a Chi in the Dots suit (B2, B3, B4) and maintain flexibility in the Characters suit.\nReason: Discarding W6 avoids disrupting the B2-B3-B4 sequence needed for a Chi and retains T2 and T3 for a possible Chi if T4 is drawn. This supports forming additional melds while maintaining a pair of T5 (from the meld and hand tile) for a winning hand.\nAnswer: Play W6"}
